1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:14,788
Imagine the house of the LORD at Shiloh, in the middle of the night, a long time
ago. The old priest in the bed cannot see.

2
00:00:14,838 --> 00:00:18,710
A boy has just run to his bedside for the third time.

3
00:00:21,760 --> 00:00:23,950
[Samuel] Here I am, for you called me.

4
00:00:25,160 --> 00:00:35,030
[Eli] Go and lie down. And if He calls you, say, Speak, LORD, for Your servant is
listening.

5
00:00:37,330 --> 00:00:48,996
He did not call. He knows he did not call. And the boy is sure someone did, three
times now, in a voice the boy takes for his.

6
00:00:49,046 --> 00:00:54,960
Here is Brian, by way of Mister Scaredy Cat, on what a blind man hears in that.

7
00:00:55,510 --> 00:01:02,733
[Brian] A blind man keeps a very short list of the voices that can be in his house,
and he knows whether each one is in the room or not.

8
00:01:02,783 --> 00:01:12,657
[Brian] So when someone insists he said a thing he did not say, he does not doubt
himself for long. He starts asking who else is here. That is what Eli did.

9
00:01:12,707 --> 00:01:21,438
[Brian] Think about the two of them in that house. The boy had his ears and he had
his eyes, and he still got it wrong, three times over. Not because he was slow.

10
00:01:21,488 --> 00:01:28,586
[Brian] Because he had never heard that voice before, and when you hear a voice you
have never heard, you reach for the nearest name you know.

11
00:01:28,636 --> 00:01:35,975
[Brian] Eli was the nearest name he knew. Now the old man. He could not see the boy
coming. By the end he probably could barely hear him.

12
00:01:36,025 --> 00:01:43,497
[Brian] And he was the one who worked out who had been calling. Not because his ears
were better. He had something the boy did not have yet.

13
00:01:43,547 --> 00:01:55,177
[Brian] He had been in that house a long time, and he knew what did not add up. So I
want to be careful how I say this. Eli never heard the voice. He still knew whose it
was.

14
00:01:55,227 --> 00:01:58,900
[Brian] Those are two different things, and the second one is what this whole
episode is about.

15
00:02:00,575 --> 00:02:11,195
This is Not By Sight, a series on blindness in the Bible, made by a blind Christian
for blind believers, their families, and the church.

16
00:02:11,245 --> 00:02:21,920
The Scripture you will hear is the Berean Standard Bible, read word for word. The
thoughts marked as Brian's are Brian Clark's own.

17
00:02:22,970 --> 00:02:33,905
This episode is part of our series on sight lost and not given back: six people in
the Bible who lost their sight and lived the rest of their lives without it.

18
00:02:33,955 --> 00:02:50,458
We tell them in three pairs. Two men blinded by their enemies, Zedekiah and Samson.
A father and a son, Isaac and Jacob. Two old men of Shiloh, Eli and Ahijah.

19
00:02:50,508 --> 00:02:55,701
Every episode begins with sounds, and with what a blind man hears in them.

20
00:02:55,751 --> 00:03:07,590
Along the way we follow the Hebrew words the Bible uses for eyes that fail, one word
handing on to the next. When we imagine a scene, we will tell you so.

21
00:03:07,640 --> 00:03:15,800
Everything else is from the text, from the old books read in their own voice, and
from Brian.

22
00:03:18,850 --> 00:03:29,541
First, where we are. Eli was the priest at Shiloh, where the tabernacle had stood
since Joshua set it up, and he judged Israel for forty years.

23
00:03:29,591 --> 00:03:35,939
That is a long time to carry a people, and the Bible gives him the title without a
word against it.

24
00:03:35,989 --> 00:03:41,196
The first time we meet him he is sitting on his chair by the doorpost of the house
of the LORD.

25
00:03:41,246 --> 00:03:47,592
A woman has come up from the hill country with her husband for the yearly sacrifice.
Her name is Hannah.

26
00:03:47,642 --> 00:03:55,670
She has no children, and the other wife in the house has them, and has made sure
Hannah feels it, year after year.

27
00:03:55,720 --> 00:04:08,679
So Hannah has gone to the house of the LORD alone, and she is weeping, and she is
praying with her lips moving and no sound coming out, and she is making a vow:

28
00:04:08,729 --> 00:04:13,964
if God gives her a son, she will give him back, for all his days.

29
00:04:14,014 --> 00:04:20,379
Eli watches her mouth move. He decides she is drunk, and he tells her so.

30
00:04:20,429 --> 00:04:31,384
That is a sighted man’s mistake, and it is worth noticing, because by the chapter we
are reading his sight has gone. But notice too what he does next.

31
00:04:31,434 --> 00:04:44,894
She answers him, quietly, and he hears her. He does not argue. He blesses her: go in
peace, and may the God of Israel grant what you have asked of Him.

32
00:04:44,944 --> 00:04:50,517
And she goes away and eats, and her face is not sad any more.

33
00:04:50,567 --> 00:05:02,724
She had come in with nothing from a priest but a wrong word, and she went out
carrying his blessing, and the next year she had Samuel. She named him for the
asking.

34
00:05:02,774 --> 00:05:15,062
When he was weaned she brought him up to Shiloh with a bull and flour and wine,
reminded Eli who she was, the woman who stood here praying, and left the boy with
him.

35
00:05:15,112 --> 00:05:21,404
Every year after that she came back with a little robe she had made, a size bigger
each time.

36
00:05:21,454 --> 00:05:31,479
And every year Eli blessed her and her husband, and asked God to give her more
children in place of the one she had lent. He did.

37
00:05:31,529 --> 00:05:34,886
She had three more sons and two daughters.

38
00:05:34,936 --> 00:05:47,473
So Samuel grew up in Eli’s house, serving before the LORD in a small linen ephod,
and the text says he grew in stature and in favour with God and with men.

39
00:05:47,523 --> 00:05:54,320
Somebody raised him. It was not his mother, who saw him once a year. It was the old
priest.

40
00:05:55,370 --> 00:06:03,537
Eli’s own two sons were another matter. Hophni and Phinehas were priests under him,
and they were wicked men.

41
00:06:03,587 --> 00:06:13,308
They took what was not theirs from the sacrifices and they did worse than that at
the door of the tent. Eli heard about it, the text says so.

42
00:06:13,358 --> 00:06:24,249
He was old by then, and he spoke to them, and he asked them the question any father
would, why do you do these things, and they did not listen.

43
00:06:24,299 --> 00:06:35,741
A man of God came and told him that both of them would die on the same day. And hear
the rest of what that man said, because it is about Eli himself.

44
00:06:35,791 --> 00:06:52,052
Whoever in your house I do not cut off, the LORD said, I will cause your eyes to
fail and your heart to grieve. Your eyes. Your heart. Eli was the one not cut off.

45
00:06:52,102 --> 00:07:03,291
He lived to ninety-eight. So from that day he knew two things were coming to him,
not only to his sons, and keep that in mind when we reach the gate.

46
00:07:03,341 --> 00:07:12,593
Eli did not stop his sons. That is the charge against him, and the Bible does not
soften it. But hold it next to the other things.

47
00:07:12,643 --> 00:07:19,948
He was a father who could not govern his sons and a priest who could govern Israel
for forty years.

48
00:07:19,998 --> 00:07:33,119
He was unkind to a stranger for one sentence and kind to her for the rest of her
life. He failed with the sons he was given and he succeeded with the son he was
lent.

49
00:07:33,169 --> 00:07:44,552
And when the boy he raised brought him the hardest word of his life, in the chapter
we are about to read, his whole answer was this: He is the LORD.

50
00:07:44,602 --> 00:07:57,590
Let Him do what is good in His eyes. Samuel is a boy in this chapter. Josephus says
he was twelve. And the word of the LORD, the text says, was rare in those days.

51
00:07:57,640 --> 00:08:05,982
Where this is. Stand at Shiloh, in the hill country of Ephraim, and face north, with
north at twelve o'clock.

52
00:08:06,032 --> 00:08:15,726
Shiloh is the first place in this series where we will stand twice, because the next
episode happens here too, about a hundred and seventy years later.

53
00:08:15,776 --> 00:08:19,770
Bethel is about half a day's walk behind you at seven o'clock.

54
00:08:19,820 --> 00:08:27,040
The hill of Jerusalem, which is still a Jebusite town and not yet David's, is a
day's walk at six o'clock.

55
00:08:27,090 --> 00:08:38,094
And at nine o'clock, a day's walk west toward the coast, is Aphek, where the
Philistine army is camped, and across from it a place called Ebenezer,

56
00:08:38,144 --> 00:08:39,823
where Israel's army is.

57
00:08:39,873 --> 00:08:43,880
Keep nine o'clock in mind. That is the direction the runner comes from.

58
00:08:50,430 --> 00:08:53,580
First Samuel chapter 3, verses 1 to 10.

59
00:08:54,130 --> 00:09:04,057
[Reader] And the boy Samuel ministered to the LORD before Eli. Now in those days the
word of the LORD was rare, and visions were scarce.

60
00:09:04,107 --> 00:09:11,571
[Reader] And at that time Eli, whose eyesight had grown so dim that he could not
see, was lying in his room.

61
00:09:11,621 --> 00:09:20,023
[Reader] Before the lamp of God had gone out, Samuel was lying down in the temple of
the LORD, where the ark of God was located.

62
00:09:20,073 --> 00:09:31,990
[Reader] Then the LORD called to Samuel, and he answered, “Here I am.” He ran to Eli
and said, “Here I am, for you have called me.” “I did not call,” Eli replied.

63
00:09:32,040 --> 00:09:45,502
[Reader] “Go back and lie down.” So he went and lay down. Once again the LORD
called, “Samuel!” So Samuel got up, went to Eli, and said, “Here I am, for you have
called me.”

64
00:09:45,552 --> 00:09:57,312
[Reader] “My son, I did not call,” Eli replied. “Go back and lie down.” Now Samuel
did not yet know the LORD, because the word of the LORD had not yet been revealed to
him.

65
00:09:57,362 --> 00:10:07,409
[Reader] Once again, for the third time, the LORD called to Samuel. He got up, went
to Eli, and said, “Here I am, for you have called me.”

66
00:10:07,459 --> 00:10:11,899
[Reader] Then Eli realized that it was the LORD who was calling the boy.

67
00:10:11,949 --> 00:10:23,585
[Reader] “Go and lie down,” he said to Samuel, “and if He calls you, say, ‘Speak,
LORD, for Your servant is listening.’” So Samuel went and lay down in his place.

68
00:10:23,635 --> 00:10:33,120
[Reader] Then the LORD came and stood there, calling as before, “Samuel! Samuel!”
And Samuel answered, “Speak, for Your servant is listening.”

69
00:10:34,306 --> 00:10:39,459
Before the lamp of God had gone out. That is a clock.

70
00:10:39,509 --> 00:10:50,227
The lamp in the holy place was lit at evening and burned until morning, so this is
the small hours, the lamp low but not out.

71
00:10:50,277 --> 00:10:58,093
At least three people are in the house that night. The boy, who can see the lamp.
The LORD, who is calling.

72
00:10:58,143 --> 00:11:10,092
And an old man in a dark room who cannot see the lamp or anything else, and who, on
the third run of small feet, is the one who understands.

73
00:11:10,142 --> 00:11:16,117
The text does not say he heard the voice. It says he realized.

74
00:11:16,167 --> 00:11:29,601
He put together a boy who was certain, a house where no one else was, and a God who
had been silent a long time, and he got there without his eyes and without his ears.

75
00:11:29,651 --> 00:11:40,440
Then he told Samuel a thing that is worth an episode unto itself. He told the boy
how to answer. Continuing with verses 11 to 18.

76
00:11:41,740 --> 00:11:50,233
[Reader] Then the LORD said to Samuel, “I am about to do something in Israel at
which the ears of all who hear it will tingle.

77
00:11:50,283 --> 00:11:58,219
[Reader] On that day I will carry out against Eli everything I have spoken about his
house, from beginning to end.

78
00:11:58,269 --> 00:12:08,543
[Reader] I told him that I would judge his house forever for the iniquity of which
he knows, because his sons blasphemed God and he did not restrain them.

79
00:12:08,593 --> 00:12:18,015
[Reader] Therefore I have sworn to the house of Eli, ‘The iniquity of Eli’s house
shall never be atoned for by sacrifice or offering.’”

80
00:12:18,065 --> 00:12:23,900
[Reader] Samuel lay down until the morning; then he opened the doors of the house of
the LORD.

81
00:12:23,950 --> 00:12:37,206
[Reader] He was afraid to tell Eli the vision, but Eli called to him and said,
“Samuel, my son.” “Here I am,” answered Samuel. “What was the message He gave you?”
Eli asked.

82
00:12:37,256 --> 00:12:45,889
[Reader] “Do not hide it from me. May God punish you, and ever so severely, if you
hide from me anything He said to you.”

83
00:12:45,939 --> 00:12:56,170
[Reader] So Samuel told him everything and did not hide a thing from him. “He is the
LORD,” replied Eli. “Let Him do what is good in His eyes.”

84
00:12:57,220 --> 00:13:10,455
Do not hide it from me. A blind man said that, to a boy who had just opened the
doors on a morning he could not see, and who was keeping something back.

85
00:13:10,505 --> 00:13:20,523
Eli could not read the boy's face. He could hear the silence, and he knew what
silence from a child who is usually full of words means.

86
00:13:20,573 --> 00:13:32,689
So he made him say all of it, under oath, fearing it would be about his own house.
And when he had heard the worst, he handed it back. He is the LORD.

87
00:13:32,739 --> 00:13:41,570
Let Him do what is good in His eyes. In His eyes. The old man whose own had gone
said that.

88
00:13:43,570 --> 00:13:55,065
The word came true some years later, in one day, and the text puts Eli in a chair
for it. Now imagine the gate at Shiloh on that afternoon.

89
00:13:55,115 --> 00:14:05,280
The text gives the frame: a man ran from the battle line all the way to Shiloh, and
when he arrived, there was Eli, sitting on his chair beside the road.

90
00:14:07,330 --> 00:14:09,027
He is ninety-eight.

91
00:14:09,077 --> 00:14:15,611
He has had himself carried to the gate and set down by the road, on the side the
runner would come from,

92
00:14:15,661 --> 00:14:20,485
because the ark has gone out to the battle and his heart is trembling for it.

93
00:14:20,535 --> 00:14:33,312
He cannot see the road. What he can do is listen to it, and a road tells a blind man
a great deal. All afternoon it has said nothing.

94
00:14:33,362 --> 00:14:45,033
Then it says one man, running, from nine o'clock, and running is rarely good news.
The feet go past him into the town. There is a pause.

95
00:14:45,083 --> 00:14:53,280
And then the whole town cries out at once, and the old man by the road is the only
person in Shiloh who does not know why.

96
00:14:57,477 --> 00:14:59,040
[Eli] Why this commotion?

97
00:15:00,211 --> 00:15:07,984
He has to ask. Everyone around him can see the runner's torn clothes and the dirt on
his head and already knows.

98
00:15:08,034 --> 00:15:17,880
He heard it all arrive, in order, before any of them did, and he is told last. First
Samuel chapter 4, verses 12 to 18.

99
00:15:20,430 --> 00:15:30,159
[Reader] That same day a Benjamite ran from the battle line all the way to Shiloh,
with his clothes torn and dirt on his head.

100
00:15:30,209 --> 00:15:39,132
[Reader] When he arrived, there was Eli, sitting on his chair beside the road and
watching, because his heart trembled for the ark of God.

101
00:15:39,182 --> 00:15:48,532
[Reader] When the man entered the city to give a report, the whole city cried out.
Eli heard the outcry and asked, “Why this commotion?”

102
00:15:48,582 --> 00:15:59,164
[Reader] So the man hurried over and reported to Eli. Now Eli was ninety-eight years
old, and his gaze was fixed because he could not see.

103
00:15:59,214 --> 00:16:07,806
[Reader] “I have just come from the battle,” the man said to Eli. “I fled from there
today.” “What happened, my son?” Eli asked.

104
00:16:07,856 --> 00:16:15,797
[Reader] The messenger answered, “Israel has fled before the Philistines, and there
has been a great slaughter among the people.

105
00:16:15,847 --> 00:16:23,229
[Reader] Your two sons, Hophni and Phinehas, are both dead, and the ark of God has
been captured.”

106
00:16:23,279 --> 00:16:34,097
[Reader] As soon as the ark of God was mentioned, Eli fell backward from his chair
by the city gate, and being old and heavy, he broke his neck and died.

107
00:16:34,147 --> 00:16:37,020
[Reader] And Eli had judged Israel forty years.

108
00:16:37,820 --> 00:16:48,029
Sitting beside the road and watching. The text says watching, of a man it says in
the next breath could not see.

109
00:16:48,079 --> 00:17:00,239
He was watching the only way left to him, and the runner gave him the news in the
order a soldier would: the army, the people, the sons, the ark.

110
00:17:00,289 --> 00:17:11,933
Three of those horrors he had been told about years before, in the night, by a boy.
He took all three and stayed in his chair. It was the last that killed him.

111
00:17:11,983 --> 00:17:15,690
Not the news about his sons. It was the loss of the ark.

112
00:17:16,990 --> 00:17:18,860
Brian, what do you hear in this one?

113
00:17:19,160 --> 00:17:27,093
[Brian] Eli is the one in this series I would least like to be. Not because of how
he went blind, which was slowly, with age, the way most people do.

114
00:17:27,143 --> 00:17:28,887
[Brian] Because of the last scene.

115
00:17:28,937 --> 00:17:36,564
[Brian] He is ninety-eight, he cannot see, and he is sitting in a chair by the road
waiting for news that everyone around him will get before he does.

116
00:17:36,614 --> 00:17:44,551
[Brian] The text says his heart trembled for the ark. He could do nothing about the
battle, nothing about his sons, nothing but sit there and listen for it.

117
00:17:44,601 --> 00:17:53,249
[Brian] That is the part of blindness that nobody warns you about: the patience you
have to have whether you want it or not. Sighted people choose when to be patient.

118
00:17:53,299 --> 00:18:02,150
[Brian] A blind person waits for the ride, waits for the person who said they would
lend their eyeballs, waits to be told what everybody else in the room already knows.

119
00:18:02,200 --> 00:18:07,933
[Brian] The city cried out before anyone told Eli why, and he had to ask. Why this
commotion?

120
00:18:07,983 --> 00:18:13,467
[Brian] I have asked that question, in those words or near enough, more times than I
can count.

121
00:18:13,517 --> 00:18:20,883
[Brian] Something has happened, the room knows it, and you are the last to be told,
and usually the telling is the gentle version.

122
00:18:20,933 --> 00:18:28,131
[Brian] Eli got the straight version, and the last thing in it was the ark. Quite
often, the frustration is not the waiting itself.

123
00:18:28,181 --> 00:18:33,961
[Brian] It is not being able to do anything with what you hear. Eli had heard for
years what his sons were doing.

124
00:18:34,011 --> 00:18:38,400
[Brian] The text says so: I hear about your wicked deeds from all these people.

125
00:18:38,450 --> 00:18:44,933
[Brian] He spoke to them and they would not listen, and the man of God came, and
then the boy came in the night with the same word again.

126
00:18:44,983 --> 00:18:54,253
[Brian] A blind man hears a great deal he cannot act on. I know the sound of a
problem starting long before I can get to it, and getting to it is the whole
difficulty.

127
00:18:54,303 --> 00:19:00,765
[Brian] There are all kinds of things I hear that people think I do not. Usually it
is when a person does not want to share.

128
00:19:00,815 --> 00:19:06,376
[Brian] My wife Barb has a disability of her own, and it puts limits on a heart that
was made for serving people.

129
00:19:06,426 --> 00:19:12,719
[Brian] Some nights I hear her crying softly behind a door in another room, over
what she can no longer do for others.

130
00:19:12,769 --> 00:19:18,614
[Brian] Jimmy, who lives with us, spent years doing God's work and should be
enjoying a peaceful retirement.

131
00:19:18,664 --> 00:19:28,241
[Brian] Instead he has a job that wears his body down, and when he thinks the house
is asleep I hear the muffled grunts of a tired man in pain. Neither of them is
telling me.

132
00:19:28,291 --> 00:19:36,805
[Brian] I hear it anyway. A sighted person can look away from something and believe
it has not been seen. There is no looking away from a sound.

133
00:19:36,855 --> 00:19:42,470
[Brian] So you carry what you heard, and you decide whether to say so, and that is
its own kind of patience.

134
00:19:45,020 --> 00:19:48,484
[Brian] What I want to hold onto from Eli is the night, not the gate.

135
00:19:48,534 --> 00:19:55,916
[Brian] His eyes had gone dim and the word of the LORD was rare, and when the voice
came it was the blind old priest, not the sighted boy,

136
00:19:55,966 --> 00:19:58,832
[Brian] who knew what it was and told Samuel how to answer.

137
00:19:58,882 --> 00:20:07,654
[Brian] In the morning he made the boy tell him all of it, though it was a judgment
on his own house, and he did not argue with it. He is the LORD, he said.

138
00:20:07,704 --> 00:20:09,690
[Brian] Let Him do what is good in His eyes.

139
00:20:11,240 --> 00:20:17,813
Now the old books. Matthew Henry is hard on Eli, harder than the text is.

140
00:20:17,863 --> 00:20:29,849
He reads the dim eyes of chapter 3 as a judgment, an affliction that came justly on
him, he says, for winking at his sons' faults. The text does not say that.

141
00:20:29,899 --> 00:20:37,485
It says his eyes had grown dim, the ordinary way, and leaves it there, and we leave
it there too.

142
00:20:37,535 --> 00:20:45,590
But Henry read the gate scene with care, and he noticed what Brian noticed, that the
old man was listening to the town.

143
00:20:46,140 --> 00:20:55,712
[Matthew Henry] Though he could not see, he could hear the tumult and crying of the
city, and perceived it to be the voice of lamentation, and mourning, and woe;

144
00:20:55,762 --> 00:21:00,490
[Matthew Henry] like a careful magistrate, he asks, What means the noise of this
tumult?

145
00:21:01,540 --> 00:21:09,870
And he noticed where the blow landed. Eli does not interrupt the messenger with any
lament for his sons, Henry says.

146
00:21:09,920 --> 00:21:21,482
There is a greater concern on his spirit, which swallows up the less. On the night
scene, Henry has one phrase for Eli's answer to the boy that is worth keeping.

147
00:21:21,532 --> 00:21:36,699
He composes himself, Henry writes, into a humble resignation to God's will, like
Aaron, who held his peace. It is the Lord. From whose bar there lies no appeal.

148
00:21:36,749 --> 00:21:43,650
Henry heard submission in it. Brian hears something a little different, and he will
say what at the end.

149
00:21:45,200 --> 00:22:03,628
Now the word. Each of these six stories has its own word for eyes that fail, and
they make a chain. Zedekiah's was "ah-VAR," blinded. Isaac's was "kah-HAH," grown
dim.

150
00:22:03,678 --> 00:22:14,857
Jacob's was "kah-VED," grown heavy. Eli gets two, one at each end of his story, and
the first is Isaac's again.

151
00:22:14,907 --> 00:22:26,674
Chapter 3 says his eyes had begun to be "kay-HEH," dim, the same root as Isaac's, in
the form that describes the eye rather than what happened to it.

152
00:22:26,724 --> 00:22:35,778
It is a rare little word, nine times in the Bible, and most of them are in
Leviticus, for a spot on the skin that is fading.

153
00:22:35,828 --> 00:22:48,770
But one of the others is in Isaiah, and it is the one to remember. A bruised reed He
will not break, Isaiah says, and a smouldering wick He will not put out.

154
00:22:48,820 --> 00:22:53,940
Smouldering is this word. A dim wick.

155
00:22:53,990 --> 00:23:07,284
In a chapter that opens with a lamp not yet gone out and an old man whose eyes had,
the Bible's word for his eyes is the word for a wick the LORD does not put out.

156
00:23:07,334 --> 00:23:19,606
The second word comes at the gate, and it hands on to the next episode. Chapter 4
says Eli was ninety-eight and his eyes "KOOM," stood.

157
00:23:19,656 --> 00:23:28,744
The translations give you fixed, or set, or dim, because it is an odd thing to say
of eyes.

158
00:23:28,794 --> 00:23:39,749
It is the plainest verb in Hebrew, to rise, to stand up, to stand still, used
hundreds of times for people getting to their feet.

159
00:23:39,799 --> 00:23:47,279
Here it is eyes that have stood still. Eyes that have stopped moving.

160
00:23:47,329 --> 00:23:57,432
It is the one description of blindness in this series written from the outside, what
a person saw when they looked at the old man's face,

161
00:23:57,482 --> 00:24:04,324
and it is used of exactly one other man in the Bible: the other old man of Shiloh,
next time.

162
00:24:04,374 --> 00:24:13,877
And set it beside the man of God's sentence from years before, your eyes will fail
and your heart will grieve.

163
00:24:13,927 --> 00:24:25,958
At the gate the text gives you both in two verses: his heart trembled for the ark,
and his eyes had stood still. He had been told.

164
00:24:26,008 --> 00:24:30,030
He sat down in that chair, by the gate, knowing it.

165
00:24:32,330 --> 00:24:39,338
[Brian] There is something in that word that only a blind person would notice. Eyes
that have stopped moving are what other people see.

166
00:24:39,388 --> 00:24:48,522
[Brian] Sighted people read the eyes, all the time, without knowing they do it, and
when a blind man's eyes still move a little they treat him as someone who sees a
little.

167
00:24:48,572 --> 00:24:55,876
[Brian] When the eyes stop following them, they decide he is totally blind, and
everything changes. The voice changes.

168
00:24:55,926 --> 00:25:04,604
[Brian] They start telling you things they would not have told you, or they stop
telling you things, and they talk about you across the room as if you had left it,

169
00:25:04,654 --> 00:25:06,800
[Brian] or were never there in the first place.

170
00:25:06,850 --> 00:25:13,080
[Brian] Eli's eyes had stood still. Everyone at that gate knew what he was. And the
runner went straight past him.

171
00:25:13,880 --> 00:25:29,532
What came after. The same hour, Eli's daughter-in-law went into labour at the news
and named the child Ichabod, the glory has departed, because the ark had been taken.

172
00:25:29,582 --> 00:25:41,760
The ark never came back to Shiloh. The Philistines carried it off, and when they
could not bear to keep it they sent it elsewhere, and the town on the hill went
quiet.

173
00:25:41,810 --> 00:25:49,560
Centuries later Jeremiah would tell Jerusalem, go to Shiloh and see what I did to
it.

174
00:25:49,610 --> 00:25:59,207
The gate where Eli sat is the last thing the Bible shows happening there, except for
one, which we will see in the next episode.

175
00:25:59,257 --> 00:26:10,230
But the night came to something too, and the chapter tells you what before it gets
to the gate. First Samuel chapter 3, verses 19 to 21.

176
00:26:10,780 --> 00:26:18,159
[Reader] And Samuel grew, and the LORD was with him, and He let none of Samuel’s
words fall to the ground.

177
00:26:18,209 --> 00:26:25,934
[Reader] So all Israel from Dan to Beersheba knew that Samuel was confirmed as a
prophet of the LORD.

178
00:26:25,984 --> 00:26:31,770
[Reader] And the LORD continued to appear at Shiloh, because there He revealed
Himself to Samuel by His word.

179
00:26:32,695 --> 00:26:44,365
The word of the LORD was rare, the chapter began. By the end of it the LORD
continued to appear at Shiloh, and the man who opened that door was not the prophet.

180
00:26:44,415 --> 00:26:49,329
It was the blind priest who told a frightened boy what to say.

181
00:26:49,379 --> 00:26:57,333
Everything the LORD said to Israel through Samuel for the rest of his long life went
through a sentence Eli taught him in the dark.

182
00:26:57,383 --> 00:27:06,600
Speak, LORD, for Your servant is listening. The old man's house ended. The thing he
said in the night did not.

183
00:27:08,400 --> 00:27:10,110
Brian, with the last word.

184
00:27:10,660 --> 00:27:18,586
[Brian] I have sat with Eli's answer for a while. He could not change what was
coming. He did not pretend he could, and he did not pretend it was not coming
either.

185
00:27:18,636 --> 00:27:28,080
[Brian] He heard the worst said plainly and handed it back to God. There is a
patience that sits by the road because it has no choice, and I know that one well.

186
00:27:28,130 --> 00:27:37,010
[Brian] There is another that hears everything, carries it, and still says He is the
LORD. That is the one I am still learning, and it is the one I want from this story.

187
00:27:39,060 --> 00:27:44,274
Next time, the same town, about a hundred and seventy years on.

188
00:27:44,324 --> 00:27:54,864
A woman walks from Tirzah to Shiloh dressed as someone she is not, carrying bread
and honey, counting on no one knowing her face.

189
00:27:54,914 --> 00:28:06,130
The old man in the house she is walking to has no use for her face. He hears her
feet at the door and says her name. Ahijah, and the disguised queen.

190
00:28:08,080 --> 00:28:16,650
This has been Not By Sight. The full story, with every source named, is at
blindnessinthebible.com.

191
00:28:16,700 --> 00:28:27,408
Scripture quotations are from the Holy Bible, Berean Standard Bible, which is in the
public domain. The scenes you heard marked as imagined are our own.

192
00:28:27,458 --> 00:28:34,830
Everything else is from the text and the old books. For we walk by faith, not by
sight.

